sections.s2_technical:
A value object has no independent lifecycle or identity; it is wholly owned by its entity. Money (amount + currency), an address, a date range, or audit stamps (created/updated) are classic examples. Modelling them as `@Embeddable` keeps the domain clean (a reusable `Money` type) while storing the fields as plain columns on the owner's table — no join, no separate id.

```java
@Embeddable
public class Address {
    private String street;
    private String city;
    private String pincode;
    // value objects are typically immutable with equals/hashCode on all fields
}

@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id private String id;
    @Embedded private Address address;   // street, city, pincode columns on customers
}
```

The embeddable's fields become columns of `customers`, so a single row holds both the customer and the address. Because an embeddable is a value object, equals and hashCode should be based on all its fields, and it is best kept immutable.

The clash arises when one entity embeds the same type twice:

```java
@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id private String id;
    @Embedded private Address homeAddress;    // wants street, city, pincode
    @Embedded private Address officeAddress;  // ALSO wants street, city, pincode → CLASH
}
```

Both embeddeds default to the same column names, which is invalid. `@AttributeOverride` remaps one (or both):

```java
@Embedded
private Address homeAddress;   // defaults: street, city, pincode

@Embedded
@AttributeOverrides({
    @AttributeOverride(name = "street",  column = @Column(name = "office_street")),
    @AttributeOverride(name = "city",    column = @Column(name = "office_city")),
    @AttributeOverride(name = "pincode", column = @Column(name = "office_pincode"))
})
private Address officeAddress;
```

Now home address uses the plain columns and office address uses the prefixed columns in the same table.

Senior nuances: embeddables can be nested (an embeddable containing another embeddable), and overrides can reach nested paths with dotted names. You can override `@AssociationOverride` for relationships inside an embeddable. Embeddables are great for a reusable `Money` value object enforcing BigDecimal + currency invariants. A `@Embedded` field that is entirely null can be represented as all-null columns, so be careful distinguishing "no address" from "address with null fields." And embeddables share the owner's table, so they are loaded eagerly with the entity (no lazy loading of an embedded value).

